{"results":[{"entries":[{"dbId":"5693181","stId":"R-HSA-5693181","id":"R-HSA-5693181","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>","type":"Protein","exactType":"ReferenceGeneProduct","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceName":"LIFR","referenceIdentifier":"P42702","compartmentNames":["plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"UniProt","referenceURL":"http://purl.uniprot.org/uniprot/P42702","disease":false}],"typeName":"Protein","entriesCount":1,"rowCount":1},{"entries":[{"dbId":"9827615","stId":"R-HSA-9827615","id":"R-HSA-9827615","name":"GATA6-<span class=\"highlighting\" >AS1</span> lncRNA","type":"RNA Sequence","exactType":"ReferenceRNASequence","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceIdentifier":"ENSEMBL:ENST00000579431","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"ENSEMBL","referenceURL":"https://www.ensembl.org/id/ENST00000579431","disease":false}],"typeName":"RNA Sequence","entriesCount":1,"rowCount":1},{"entries":[{"dbId":"6783585","stId":"R-HSA-6783585","id":"R-HSA-6783585","name":"OSM:OSMR,<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9823969","stId":"R-HSA-9823969","id":"R-HSA-9823969","name":"p-2S-SMAD2,3:GATA6-<span class=\"highlighting\" >AS1</span> lncRNA:GATA6 gene","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"1445125","stId":"R-HSA-1445125","id":"R-HSA-1445125","name":"AS160:IRAP","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["cytoplasmic vesicle membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0030659"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"1445126","stId":"R-HSA-1445126","id":"R-HSA-1445126","name":"Phospho AS160:14-3-3","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["cytoplasmic vesicle membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0030659"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"1445133","stId":"R-HSA-1445133","id":"R-HSA-1445133","name":"p-5S,T642-AS160:IRAP","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"AS160 (TBC1D4) phosphorylated on serines 318, 341, 570, 588, and 751 and threonine 642 binds to all 14-3-3 proteins, although binding to 14-3-3 delta (YWHAZ) is comparatively low (Ramm et al. 2006, Howlett et al. 2007, Ngo et al. 2009, Treebak et al. 2009, Koumanov et al. 2011). As inferred from mouse, binding to 14-3-3 does not interfere with the interaction between AS160 and IRAP (LNPEP).","compartmentNames":["cytoplasmic vesicle membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0030659"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"1445124","stId":"R-HSA-1445124","id":"R-HSA-1445124","name":"p-5S,T642-AS160:14-3-3:IRAP","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["cytoplasmic vesicle membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0030659"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"6783533","stId":"R-HSA-6783533","id":"R-HSA-6783533","name":"CTF1:LIFR:JAKs","type":"Complex","exactType":"Complex","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Complex","entriesCount":7,"rowCount":7},{"entries":[{"dbId":"8934704","stId":"R-HSA-8934704","id":"R-HSA-8934704","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> gene","type":"DNA Sequence","exactType":"ReferenceDNASequence","species":["Homo sapiens"],"referenceIdentifier":"ENSEMBL:ENSG00000113594","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":true,"databaseName":"ENSEMBL","referenceURL":"https://www.ensembl.org/id/ENSG00000113594","disease":false}],"typeName":"DNA Sequence","entriesCount":1,"rowCount":1},{"entries":[{"dbId":"8934690","stId":"R-HSA-8934690","id":"R-HSA-8934690","name":"<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> gene expression","type":"Reaction","exactType":"omitted","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Binding of the RUNX1:CBFB complex to the promoter of the <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> gene stimulates <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> transcription. In contrast, the oncogenic fusion protein RUNX1-ETO, the product of t(8;21) translocation in acute myeloid leukemia (AML), represses transcription of the <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> gene (Qadi et al. 2016). <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> encodes the receptor for the leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF), a member of the interleukin-6 (IL6) cytokine family. Signaling by <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> is implicated in hematopoiesis, embryo implantation, placental formation and nervous system development (Nicola and Babon 2015).","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm","plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654","0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"6784189","stId":"R-HSA-6784189","id":"R-HSA-6784189","name":"JAKs bind <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Leukemia inhibitory factor receptor (<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>) beta and Oncostatin M receptor (OSMR) constitutively associates with the JAK-TYK family of cytoplasmic protein tyrosine kinases in the absence of ligand. Activation of these kinases occur as a result of ligand-induced heterodimerzation of <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> beta or OSMR with gp130 (Stahl et al. 1994, Hermanns et al. 2000).","compartmentNames":["plasma membrane","cytosol"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886","0005829"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"6783681","stId":"R-HSA-6783681","id":"R-HSA-6783681","name":"LIF binds <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF), initially named for its ability to cause myeloid leukemic cells to differentiate into macrophages (Gearing et al. 1987) is a member of the interleukin-6 (IL-6)-related cytokines. It possesses overlapping function with other IL-6 family members. The pleiotrophic effects of LIF in many physiological systems include proliferation, differentiation, and cell survival (Hilton 1992, Metcalf 1992). It exerts numerous effects in the nervous system, promote gliogenesis, support neural stem cell (NSC) renewal (Bonni et al. 1997, Hermanson et al. 2002, Pitman et al. 2004) and also been implicated in the expression of various cytokines. LIF exerts its effects by binding to a bipartite membrane receptor complex that consists of the LIF receptor subunit (<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>) and the glycoprotein (gp)130 subunit and activates STAT3. LIF initially binds <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> and the <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> Ig-like domains contributes to LIF binding (Bitard et al. 2003).","compartmentNames":["plasma membrane","extracellular region"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886","0005576"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"9823959","stId":"R-HSA-9823959","id":"R-HSA-9823959","name":"SMAD2,3 and GATA6-<span class=\"highlighting\" >AS1</span> lncRNA bind the GATA6 gene","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"NODAL signaling in the anterior primitive streak causes phosphorylation of SMAD2 and SMAD3 which co-translocate with SMAD4 to the nucleus and together bind the promoter of the GATA6 gene (inferred from human pluripotent stem cells in Brown et al. 2011, Yang et al. 2020). The antisense transcript GATA6-<span class=\"highlighting\" >AS1</span> from an upstream region of the GATA6 coding region enhances the interaction between SMAD2,3 and the GATA6 promoter (Yang et al. 2020). GATA6-<span class=\"highlighting\" >AS1</span> is required for differentiation of definitive endoderm from embryonic stem cells (Yang et al. 2020).","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"8934688","stId":"R-HSA-8934688","id":"R-HSA-8934688","name":"RUNX1:CBFB binds the <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> gene promoter","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"The RUNX1:CBFB complex binds both the general and the placental promoter of the <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> gene (Qadi et al. 2016).","compartmentNames":["nucleoplasm"],"compartmentAccession":["0005654"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"6783556","stId":"R-HSA-6783556","id":"R-HSA-6783556","name":"LIFR:JAKs bind gp130:JAKs:CNTFR","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"The cytokine receptor gp130 is the shared signalling subunit of the interleukin (IL)-6-type cytokines. IL-6 and IL-11 signal through gp130 homodimer whereas leukaemia inhibitory factor (LIF), ciliary neurotrophic factor receptor (CNTFR), Cardiotrophin-like cytokine factor 1 (CLCF1), Cardiotrophin-1 (CT-1) and Oncostatin-M (OSM) exerts its action through a heterodimer of gp130 and the LIF receptor (<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>) (Heinrich et al. 2003, Giese et al. 2005). <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> beta structure closely resembles that of gp130 and upon ligand binding forms gp130:<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> beta heterodimer. Finally CNTFR alpha complexed with this signal transducing heterodimer converts into functional tripartite CNTF receptor and induces phosphorylation of both gp130 and <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> beta (Davis et al. 1993, Stahl & Yancopoulos 1994). Formation of the tripartite complex then leads to activation of Janus family kinases, JAK1, 2, 3, and TYK2 and phosphorylation of tyrosine residues on the cytoplasmic domain of gp130 (Kass 2011).","compartmentNames":["plasma membrane","extracellular region"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886","0005576"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"1445149","stId":"R-HSA-1445149","id":"R-HSA-1445149","name":"14-3-3 binds p-5S,T642-AS160 (TBC1D4)","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"AS160 (TBC1D4) phosphorylated on serines 318, 341, 570, 588, and 751 and threonine 642 binds to all 14-3-3 proteins, although binding to 14-3-3 delta (YWHAZ) is comparatively low (Ramm et al. 2006, Howlett et al. 2007, Ngo et al. 2009, Treebak et al. 2009, Koumanov et al. 2011). As inferred from mouse, binding to 14-3-3 does not interfere with the interaction between AS160 and IRAP (LNPEP).","compartmentNames":["cytoplasmic vesicle membrane","cytosol"],"compartmentAccession":["0030659","0005829"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"1445144","stId":"R-HSA-1445144","id":"R-HSA-1445144","name":"p-AKT1,p-AKT2 phosphorylates AS160 (TBC1D4)","type":"Reaction","exactType":"omitted","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"As inferred from mouse, AKT2 and, to a lesser extent, AKT1 phosphorylate AS160 (TBC1D4) in response to insulin signaling (Howlett et al. 2007, Karlsson et al 2005). AS160, a RAB GTPase activating protein, interacts with IRAP (LNPEP) and is associated with cytoplasmic vesicles containing GLUT4 (SLC2A4).","compartmentNames":["cytoplasmic vesicle membrane","cytosol"],"compartmentAccession":["0030659","0005829"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"5696482","stId":"R-HSA-5696482","id":"R-HSA-5696482","name":"CTF1 binds LIFR:JAKs","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Cardiotrophin-1 (CTF1/CT-1) is a member of the IL-6 family of cytokines which was originally discovered as a factor which can induce hypertrophy of cardiac myocytes both in vitro and in vivo (Pennica et al. 1995a). Subsequently, CTF1 has been shown to have a wide variety of different effects on cardiac and non cardiac cells including the ability to stimulate the survival of both cardiac and neuronal cells (Latchman 1999). Leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF), CTF1, and oncostatin M (OSM) are four helix bundle cytokines acting through a common heterodimeric receptor composed of gp130 and LIF receptor (<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>) (Pennica et al. 1995b).","compartmentNames":["plasma membrane","extracellular region"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886","0005576"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"6783552","stId":"R-HSA-6783552","id":"R-HSA-6783552","name":"OSM binds LIFR:JAKs,OSMR:JAKs","type":"Reaction","exactType":"binding","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"Oncostatin M (OSM) is a multifunctional cytokine produced by bone microenvironment by cells of both mesenchymal and hematopoietic origin, including osteocytes, osteoblasts, macrophages and T lymphocytes (Brown et al. 1987, Malik et al. 1989). It belongs to the interleukin-6 (IL-6) subfamily and shares properties with all the members of the family but is closely related to leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF) structurally and functionally and it infact utilizes the LIF receptor (<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>) in addition to its specific receptor OSMR. In humans, there are two types of functional OSM receptor complexes: the type I OSM receptor complex consisting of gp130 and LIF receptor (<span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>) subunits, and the type II OSM receptor complex consisting of gp130 and OSM receptor beta (OSMRbeta) subunits (Chen & Benveniste 2004, Thoma et al. 1994, Gomez-Lechon 1999). Upon association with its specific receptor complexes OSM, then activates two major signaling pathways: Janus Kinase-Signal Transducers and Activators of Transcription (JAK-STAT) and Mitogen-Activated Protein Kinase (MAPK), to regulate downstream events. OSM is involved in the regulation of complex cellular processes such as growth regulation, differentiation, gene expression, and cell survival in humans (Tanaka & Miyajima 2004).","compartmentNames":["plasma membrane","extracellular region"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886","0005576"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Reaction","entriesCount":22,"rowCount":10},{"entries":[{"dbId":"6783627","stId":"R-HSA-6783627","id":"R-HSA-6783627","name":"LIFR:JAKs,OSMR:JAKs","type":"Set","exactType":"DefinedSet","species":["Homo sapiens"],"compartmentNames":["plasma membrane"],"compartmentAccession":["0005886"],"isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Set","entriesCount":1,"rowCount":1},{"entries":[{"dbId":"8939247","stId":"R-HSA-8939247","id":"R-HSA-8939247","name":"RUNX1 regulates transcription of genes involved in interleukin signaling","type":"Pathway","exactType":"Pathway","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"The RUNX1:CBFB complex regulates transcription of at least a couple of genes involved in interleukin signaling. The <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> gene, a direct transcriptional target of the RUNX1:CBFB complex (Qadi et al. 2016), encodes the receptor for the leukemia inhibitory factor (LIF), a member of the interleukin-6 family. <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span> is implicated in hematopoiesis, embryo implantation, placental formation and nervous system development (Nicola et al. 2015). In association with its co-activator ELF1, the RUNX1:CBFB complex stimulates transcription of the IL3 gene, encoding interleukin-3 (Mao et al. 1999).","isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"hasEHLD":false,"disease":false},{"dbId":"6788467","stId":"R-HSA-6788467","id":"R-HSA-6788467","name":"IL-6-type cytokine receptor ligand interactions","type":"Pathway","exactType":"Pathway","species":["Homo sapiens"],"summation":"The members involved in (interleukin)-6-type cytokine signalling are the IL-6, IL-11, LIF (leukaemia inhibitory factor), OSM (oncostatin M), ciliary neurotrophic factor (CNTF), cardiotrophin-1 (CTF1) and cardiotrophin-like cytokine factor 1 (CLCF1). Receptors involved in recognition of the IL-6-type cytokines can be subdivided in the non-signalling alpha-receptors (IL6R, IL 11R, and CNTFR) and the signal transducing receptors (gp130, <span class=\"highlighting\" >LIFR</span>, and OSMR). The latter associate with JAKs and become tyrosine phosphorylated in response to cytokine stimulation (Heinrich et al. 1998, 2003). IL27 and IL35 belongs to IL12 cytokine family but they share gp130 as a component of signalling receptor, along with IL-6, IL-11, LIF, OSM, CNTF, CTF1 and CLCF1.","isDisease":false,"hasReferenceEntity":false,"hasEHLD":false,"disease":false}],"typeName":"Pathway","entriesCount":2,"rowCount":2}],"rowCount":23,"numberOfGroups":7,"numberOfMatches":35}